{
  "name": "Tile-based upscale & detail refiner for FLUX.2 Klein (9B) using the 42lux Hildegard-Refiner reference-latent scheme",
  "author": "Pfannkuchensack",
  "description": "Tile-based upscale & detail refiner for FLUX.2 Klein (9B) using the 42lux\nHildegard-Refiner reference-latent scheme. Requires the Hildegard LoRA.",
  "version": "1.0",
  "contact": "",
  "tags": "upscale,klein9b",
  "notes": "FLUX.2 Klein — Hildegard Tile Refiner\n\nA tile-based upscale & detail refiner for FLUX.2 Klein, porting the 42lux\nHildegard-Refiner reference-latent scheme. It upscales the source image,\ncuts it into overlapping tiles, and regenerates each tile with FLUX.2 guided\nby three references — the tile crop, a 3x3 position map, and a global\nthumbnail — then stitches the tiles back into one image.\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n- FLUX.2 Klein 9B transformer. The Hildegard LoRA is trained for 9B and will\n  NOT load on the 4B model. A GGUF/fp8 9B works (the LoRA is applied via\n  sidecar).\n- Hildegard-Refiner LoRA (huggingface.co/42lux/hildegard) — mandatory. Without\n  it the model cannot read the reference latents.\n- FLUX.2 VAE and a Qwen3 text encoder.\n\nPROMPT\nStart the prompt with the trigger \"RFNTILE.\" followed by a short instruction\nto refine/add detail while preserving the original composition, lighting and\ncolour. Keep it generic for high tile counts to avoid hallucinated content.\n\nCONTROLS\n- Image        : source image to refine.\n- Scale Factor : how much to upscale before refining (2 = 2x).\n- Tile Size    : generation resolution per tile (1024 recommended).\n- Cell Size    : resolution of each cell in the 3x3 position map. Lower = fewer\n                 tokens / less VRAM (256 is a good balance; 512 is sharper but\n                 ~4x heavier).\n- Blend Amount : tile-overlap blending when stitching. Raise it (e.g. 128) to\n                 hide seams. Must be <= the overlap between tiles.\n\nTIPS\n- Large image x high scale = many tiles = long runtime and high VRAM. Start\n  small to validate, then scale up.\n- VRAM is driven mostly by the position-map token count — lower Cell Size if\n  you run out.\n- If seams remain visible, raise Blend Amount and/or switch the Merge node's\n  blend mode to Linear.\n",
